.TH THICKET 1 "2026-08-16" "thicket" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
thicket \- Miller-column TUI file browser for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B thicket
.RI [ path ]
.br
.B thicket
.RB { \-h | \-\-help }
.br
.B thicket
.RB { \-v | \-\-version }
.br
.B thicket
.B update
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B thicket
is a terminal file browser using a Miller-column (multi-pane) layout, similar
to macOS Finder's column view. It replaces
.BR cd (1)
and
.BR ls (1)
navigation with a visual, arrow-key-driven browser: pressing Enter changes
the calling shell's directory to the one selected, via a shell wrapper
function (see
.B SHELL INTEGRATION
below) that captures the program's stdout.
.PP
.B thicket
itself never changes the calling shell's working directory \- only the
.B th
wrapper function does, by reading the path
.B thicket
prints to stdout on exit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.RI [ path ]
Directory to start browsing in. Defaults to the current directory.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and the keybinding table to stdout, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B update
Download and install the latest release for the current OS/arch.
Reads
.B PREFIX
from the environment at the time you run it (default
.IR /usr/local ),
the same as
.IR scripts/install.sh .
If you installed to a non-default
.BR PREFIX ,
export it again before running
.BR "thicket update" ,
or the update will land under
.IR /usr/local
instead (see
.B ENVIRONMENT
below). Like the install script, includes the same sudo-elevation
behavior for a non-writable prefix. Equivalent to re-running the install script.
A start path literally named
.I update
in the current directory is shadowed by this subcommand: running
.B thicket update
always triggers the installer, never opens such a directory. Pass
.I ./update
instead to open it.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.BR Up ", " k
Move selection up.
.TP
.BR Down ", " j
Move selection down.
.TP
.BR PgUp ", " PgDn
Move selection by a full page.
.TP
.BR Home ", " End
Jump to the first/last entry.
.TP
.BR Right ", " l
Open the selected directory and move focus right. No-op on files.
.TP
.BR Left ", " h
Go up one directory and move focus left. No-op at
.IR / .
.TP
.B Enter
Choose: cd to the selected directory, or to the active directory if a
file or empty directory is selected; then exit.
.TP
.BR q ", " Esc ", " Ctrl-C
Quit without changing directory.
.TP
.B .
Toggle hidden (dotfile) visibility. Off by default.
.TP
.B r
Refresh the active directory's listing.
.TP
.B /
Type-ahead search the active column: type to jump to the first matching
entry.
.B Enter
keeps the match,
.B Esc
cancels back to where the cursor was.
.TP
.B f
Recursive find: type to filter files and directories under the active
directory (files and directories are both matched). \c
.B Enter
jumps to the match's parent directory with the cursor on the match; \c
.B Esc
cancels with no change.
.TP
.B ?
Toggle the in-app help screen (this key table). Any other key is ignored
while the help screen is open; press
.B ?
or
.B Esc
to close it.
.TP
.B m
Bookmark the active directory under a letter (\c
.BR a - z ,
.BR A - Z ).
.TP
.B `
Jump straight to a bookmarked directory by letter.
.TP
.B '
Open the full-screen marks list:
.BR Up / k
and
.BR Down / j
move,
.B Enter
jumps to the highlighted mark,
.B d
deletes it,
.BR q / Esc
closes the list.
.TP
.B y
Copy the highlighted entry's absolute path to the system clipboard
(shells out to
.BR wl-copy ,
.BR xclip ,
or
.BR xsel ,
whichever is found first); if the active directory is empty, copies its
own path instead.
.TP
.B Y
Copy the active directory's own absolute path to the system clipboard,
regardless of what is highlighted.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/thicket/marks
Persistent directory-marks table, falling back to
.I $HOME/.local/state/thicket/marks
if
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset. Each line holds one mark: a single letter, a tab character,
then the absolute directory path it points to.
If the file exists but cannot be read, thicket exits with status 2 (see
.B EXIT STATUS
below).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B THICKET_NO_UPDATE_CHECK
If set to any non-empty value, disables the on-launch check for a newer
release. Has no effect on
.B thicket update
itself, which always checks.
.TP
.B THICKET_CHANNEL
Set to
.I beta
to track pre-release builds (tagged
.IR vX.Y.Z-beta.N )
instead of only stable releases. Affects
.BR "thicket update" ,
the on-launch check, and
.IR scripts/install.sh
alike. Unset, or any value other than
.IR beta ,
tracks stable only (the default).
.TP
.B PREFIX
Used only by
.B thicket update
(and by
.IR scripts/install.sh ):
the install root for the binary and man page. Defaults to
.IR /usr/local .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A directory was selected (see
.B SHELL INTEGRATION
below); its path was printed to stdout.
.TP
.B 1
The user quit without selecting (q, Esc, or Ctrl-C).
.TP
.B 2
A setup or runtime error occurred (e.g. the starting directory was
unreadable, or the controlling terminal could not be opened). Details are
printed to stderr.
.SH SHELL INTEGRATION
.B thicket
prints the selected path to stdout and exits; it does not itself change
any shell's working directory. Source one of the provided wrapper
scripts to get a
.B th
shell function that captures that output and
.BR cd (1)
s into it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
source /path/to/thicket/shell/thicket.bash   # bash
source /path/to/thicket/shell/thicket.zsh    # zsh
.fi
.RE
.SH NON-GOALS
.B thicket
is navigation-only: no file create/rename/delete/copy/move, no config
file, no mouse support, no filesystem watching.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cd (1),
.BR ls (1)
